<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Man-Made River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">begun 1980s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Great Man-Made River Project</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Man-Made River is a large pipeline network in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, begun in the 1980s, that pumps fossil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifers</a> beneath the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> to coastal cities. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> draw a parallel between it and the ancient Garamantes' exploitation of the same non-renewable water.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-man-made-river.jpg" alt="Great Man-Made River" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Danmichaelo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Man_Made_River_schematic_EN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
